 launch being masked by a secondary cleanup error. Original counterexamples stay.
+A further four-case review found that an interruption first observed during
+cleanup could be replaced by a later ordinary wait error or cleanup timeout.
+The correction preserves that first interruption while still attempting every
+handle/descriptor close. It never turns the uncertain cleanup into success or
+changes any deadline. The original four RED cases remain in the review module.
+Another six-case review covers descriptor reuse after an interrupted close:
+remove a closing descriptor number from owned bookkeeping before closing it, so
+later cleanup cannot close an unrelated resource that reused that number. This
+preserves cancellation and fails closed; it does not certify every OS-level
+interruption point or malicious in-process isolation.
+
+The first Windows native run retained a replay-fixture failure (`captured` versus
+`already_captured`) and two setup/teardown errors on one large-input case because
+pytest embedded the200KB payload in its environment-bound case ID. The corrected
+fixture expects only the documented operation-status transition and compares all
+other fields unchanged. Short explicit IDs leave every input byte and size check
+intact; the new final native run must actually execute that case. No production
+replay rule, timeout, operating-system limit or workflow selector is relaxed.
